//! Marker interfaces and phantom type ownership.
//!
//! ## Overview
//!
//! `Copy`, `Send`, and `Sync` express type capabilities. `PhantomData<T>` carries a type relationship without storing a value of `T`.
//!
//! ## Example
//!
//! ```arandu
//! import std.core.marker as marker
//!
//! let tag = marker.phantom<int>()
//! ```
//!
//! ## Safety
//! `Send` and `Sync` are checked conservatively for owned storage. A marker
//! does not make a raw pointer, borrowed view, or destructor-bearing resource
//! safe to transfer or share across tasks.
//!
//! ## Allocations
//! `PhantomData<T>` has no runtime payload and does not allocate.
//!
//! ## Platform
//! `native` · `wasm32` · `bare-metal`

module std.core.marker

/// Zero-sized type used to mark that a struct logically owns or refers to `T`.
public struct PhantomData<T> {}

/// Creates a new `PhantomData` instance.
public func phantom<T>(): PhantomData<T> {
    return PhantomData<T> {}
}

/// Compiler-checked marker for scalar storage and aggregates whose bytes can be
/// duplicated without aliasing ownership or requiring destruction.
public interface Copy {}

/// Compiler-checked transfer bound for owned scalar storage and aggregates.
/// Views, raw pointers, coroutine state and resources with destructors are
/// conservatively rejected until their transfer contracts are supported.
/// Generic arguments participate in the proof, including PhantomData<T>.
public interface Send {}

/// Compiler-checked sharing bound for the same conservative owned-storage subset.
/// This does not establish a borrowed scope lifetime or permit concurrent mutation.
public interface Sync {}
